    "project.advent-harvest.body": "The current focus is the overworld: seed-driven procedural generation that ranges from continents to archipelagos, a biome model layered over latitude and elevation climate bands, and hex-grid pathfinding. Saves are real progress rather than bookmarks, and the systems carry a headless test suite (GUT). Stand-in art while the systems mature. In active development.",
    "project.advent-harvest.moreLabel": "milestones & screenshots",
    "project.backyard.title": "Backyard",
    "project.backyard.summary": "A backyard drawn from drone photos: a freestanding composite deck at the back slider, a 16 ft buff flagstone patio with a round fire apron and stone fire ring, a stone raised herb bed, a sound-damping screen around the AC, a stone walkway, a row of American Pillar arborvitae and a French drain.",
    "project.backyard.body": "A new 25 by 20 ft composite deck at the sliding door in the bay of a brick ranch, running from the chimney to near the house's east corner, its outer corners cut at 45 degrees, a vented composite skirt below, freestanding on two beams because a ledger cannot hang on brick veneer. The Blackstone griddle and a smoker stand along the east rail, dining by the slider under a cantilever umbrella. One stair drops off the west side onto a 16 ft square of dry-laid buff flagstone holding the outdoor sofa set, with a 14 ft round apron and a 52 in stone fire ring centred on the patio's northwest corner and an 18 in stone seat wall curving around the ring's north half and along the patio's north edge. Between the patio and the house, a stone raised herb bed stands 4 in off the brick, and the AC is screened on its north and east sides with vinyl lattice over mass-loaded vinyl, left open to the west and on top so it still breathes. Five American Pillar arborvitae stand in one row along the back fence on the slider's line, and a French drain along the wet east fence ties into the existing corner drain. Revision A5, laid out from drone photos.",
    "project.backyard.moreLabel": "drawing set & PDF",
    "project.garage-shop.title": "Garage shop",
    "project.garage-shop.summary": "A one-car woodshop in an 18 by 20 ft attached garage with a 12 ft ceiling: locking wall-hung tool cabinet, a rolling flip bench carrying the table saw, miter saw and planer, mobile dust cart, a potting station, bulk storage on the landing, and a 100 A subpanel with a dedicated circuit for every stationary tool.",
    "project.garage-shop.body": "The truck parks in the center bay with its nose over the 6 in landing that runs across the back wall, the potting station and the machine bay take the west wall with the bulk rack on the landing behind them, and the shop takes the east wall. Everything that cuts is on wheels and parks against the wall: a 92 in flip bench with the DWE7491 table saw sunk flush at one end, the DCS785 miter saw on a flip section in the middle and the DW735 planer on a flip section at the other end, each flip turning on a 1 in steel shaft in flange bearings with a riser bench top on its back, and a dust cart with a cyclone on a shop vac. A 48 in birch cabinet on a French cleat holds the cordless tools behind hardened hasps and discus padlocks, pinned to the wall from inside. A 100 A subpanel fed from the basement carries eight circuits, with a 50 A EV circuit roughed in for later. About $2,400 in priced lines, every one a checked listing. Revision B, drawn on the measured garage.",
    "project.garage-shop.moreLabel": "drawing set & PDF",
    "project.rock-screen.title": "Rock screen",
    "project.rock-screen.summary": "A tilt-tray screen for cleaning a pool's worth of river rock: shovel it onto a hardware-cloth tray at counter height, bounce, pick and rinse it, then lift a handle and it slides down a funnel into a 50 lb burlap sack on a hand truck.",
    "project.rock-screen.body": "River rock that came out from under a pool, full of roots, leaves and dirt, going back into 50 lb burlap sacks. The tray is a 24 x 48 in 2x6 frame with 1/2 in hardware cloth on 2x2 slats, hung on two carriage bolts inside a 2x4 stand and resting on hockey pucks so it can be bounced to drop the dirt. Pick the junk, hose the load, then lift the handle: the tray tilts 35 degrees and the rock slides off a flashing lip through a plywood funnel into a sack cuffed on the spout, standing on a hand truck so nothing heavy is lifted off the ground. Three shovels a sack, 45 lb, about 60 sacks a yard. About $190 in lumber and hardware. Design complete, build pending.",
